# Combined Auto-Trade Web App (Delta + Upstox)

A real web application (not just a browser dashboard) with a settings UI where every symbol's
**risk:reward ratio, quantity, timeframe, and $/₹ conversion** is fully customizable — and the
trading engine runs server-side, so it's genuinely 24/7 once deployed (no browser tab needed).

## What's different from the earlier scripts

- **One web page controls everything** — add/remove symbols, set ratio (1:1 or custom),
  set target/stoploss in $ or ₹, choose fixed quantity or auto-size from a ₹ risk amount,
  see a live preview of what each config resolves to before saving.
- **Runs as a proper server** (Flask), not a script — deployable the same way (Railway etc.),
  but now the *configuration* also happens through the browser instead of editing env vars.
- **Multi-broker structure** — Delta Exchange is fully wired up; Upstox candle/LTP reading is
  included, order placement is stubbed pending your static-IP whitelist (see note below).

## Files

- `app.py` — Flask server. Two pages: `/` (bot control panel) and `/screener` (the full EMA
  screener, Option Chain, and Market Overview — same proven tool from before, now served as
  part of this one app, with a link back and forth between the two).
- `bot_engine.py` — the trading loop (EMA crossover detection, position management) for both brokers
- `calc.py` — the $/₹ calculator: resolves each symbol's ratio_mode/target/stoploss/quantity settings
  into actual price-move targets and contract quantities
- `config_store.py` — persists your settings to `app_config.json` on disk
- `templates/index.html` — the control panel UI (settings, symbols, live bot status)
- `templates/screener.html` — the screener/option-chain/market-overview page (client-side — paste
  your Upstox token there directly in the browser, same as before; this page doesn't touch the
  server-side bot engine)
- `requirements.txt`

## Run locally

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python app.py
```

Open `http://localhost:8080` in your browser. Fill in your Delta API key/secret (testnet first!),
configure your symbols, click **Save Settings**, then **Start**.

## Deploy for 24/7 (same as before)

Push these files to your GitHub repo (same repo you used before, or a new one), connect it to
Railway, and set the **Start Command** to:

```
python app.py
```

Railway will detect the `PORT` environment variable automatically and the app will bind to it.
Once deployed, open the Railway-provided URL in your browser any time to check status or change
settings — the bot itself keeps running on Railway's server regardless of whether you have that
page open.

## How the customization works

Each symbol has these independent settings:
- **Ratio mode**: `1:1` (target and stoploss are equal) or `custom` (set them separately)
- **Target/Stoploss unit**: `$` (plain price points, like the original BTCUSD setup) or `₹`
  (rupees, auto-converted to the equivalent price move using your USD/INR rate)
- **Quantity mode**: `fixed` (exact contract count, e.g. 1) or `from_rupees` (the app computes
  the contract count so that a stoploss hit loses approximately your specified ₹ amount — rounded
  to the nearest whole contract since Delta requires integer sizes)

The settings page shows a live preview (resolved $ move, quantity, and estimated ₹ profit/loss)
before you save, so you can sanity-check the numbers.

## Upstox note (static IP)

Upstox blocks order placement without a whitelisted static IP. Candle/LTP reading works fine
without one. Once you have a static IP (Railway Pro's shared static IP, or a small VPS), update
`bot_engine.py`'s Upstox section to place real orders the same way the Delta path does — ask me
and I'll wire it in once you're ready.

## Safety

- Defaults to `dry_run: true` and Delta `testnet` — verify signals look right before flipping either.
- Never commit `app_config.json` to a public repo once it has real API keys in it.
